 ### View Extraction
-View extraction is a technique used to acquire different views from a hologram, often in the context of light field displays or reconstruction. A window with the resolution of the vertical axis of the hologram moves along the horizontal axis to acquire sub-holograms that simulate a pinhole camera view [S2]. The apertures extracted can be chosen densely enough to overlap, as increasing the number of views improves the final rendering [S2]. However, the Field of View (FOV) is crucial; bigger FOVs require more views and increased computational complexity [S2].
+View extraction is a technique used to acquire different views from a generated hologram, often for display on light field displays [S2].
+*   **Method:** A window with the resolution of the vertical axis of the hologram moves along the horizontal axis to acquire sub-holograms that simulate a pinhole camera view [S2].
+*   **Parameters:** The number of views can be chosen densely enough to overlap, as increasing the number of views improves final rendering quality [S2]. However, the targeted Field of View (FOV) is crucial; bigger FOVs require more views and increased computational complexity [S2].
+*   **Pitfalls:** Extracted apertures can produce aperture diffraction during numerical reconstruction due to sharp intensity edges of the rectangular aperture. Apodization is proposed to suppress side lobe leakage, though this may decrease resolution [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The computation time is dominated by the generation of $N$ perspective views. The number of views $N$ scales with the FOV and desired angular resolution [S2].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing $N$ views, each of size $W \times H$.
-- **Performance:** Allows computation at nearly interactive rates when implemented on specialized hardware [S1].
-- **Strengths:** High computational efficiency compared to full CGH methods; suitable for real-time or near-real-time applications [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Limited to horizontal parallax only (HPO); requires a large number of views to avoid sampling artifacts, which can limit image quality and increase computational load [S1].
+*   **Performance:** The stereogram approach allows for computation at nearly interactive rates when implemented on specialized hardware [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The primary limitation is the need for a large number of perspective views to create a high-quality image free from sampling artifacts, which limits computation speed on general-purpose hardware [S1].
+*   **Use Cases:** Suitable for applications where horizontal parallax is sufficient and interactive or near-interactive generation is required, leveraging specialized hardware [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The holographic stereogram approach was introduced as a method to achieve interactive 3D holographic displays [S1]. It was developed to overcome the computational limitations of traditional interference-based holography, which generally requires supercomputers and simple images to achieve interactive display [S1]. The method leverages the human visual system's tolerance for certain resolutions and uses specialized hardware to accelerate the rendering process [S1].
+The holographic stereogram approach is part of the broader field of computer-generated holography. It is described as a method that produces an image with resolution finer than can be utilized by the human visual system [S1]. The approach is contrasted with interference approaches that generally employ supercomputers [S1].
 
